x-inline-csharp-csharp-script.csx\nhost-classic-asp-vbscript.vbs\nhost-context.cmd\nhost-context.js\nhost-context.sh\nhost-context.vbs\nhost-elixir-phoenix-elixir.exs\nhost-google-docs-sidebar-google-apps-script.gs\nhost-hta-jscript-javascript.js\nhost-jenkins-pipeline-groovy.groovy\nhost-jsp-webshell-jsp.jsp\nhost-lua-openresty-lua.lua\nhost-perl-cgi-perl.pl\nhost-php-webshell-php.php\nhost-ruby-rack-ruby.rb\nhost-sct-scriptlet-jscript.js\nhost-wsf-jscript-jscript.js\nhost-wsf-mixed-vbscript.vbs\nimpact-plan.cmd\n# Day 7 — Node by Node (script-analysis, node operation graphs)\n\nConcept: one evidence.zip with 61 members (PowerShell samples, JScript/Batch/VBScript/Python/Bash/Node operation\ngraphs); each graph decodes to a plan (steps/adapter/campaign/indicator) and is sorted into a role.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes. TT attempt 26b7da83 failed because its download was the GM body `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` (24 B saved as evidence.zip, sha != contract sha256), so every field came out None/default. The harvested variants 49b73141 + 7d6f8d53 were already answered 469/469 by the current solver (spot-checked against the raw bytes; their expected.json is solver-derived, not GM-verified). Parse rule: if every artifact is a small `{\"error\": ...}` JSON, exit non-zero (solve.py now does) instead of guessing roles. The bytes-less case was removed from cases/ (still kept under time_trial/attempts/26b7da83).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none (Day 8 TT). The start returned 503 `training_rotation_invalid` with no attempt and no artifact, so there were no variant bytes. The solver answers the TT contract's 615 ids 615/615 on the single's bytes. Rule: a start error is an orchestrator retry, not a solver change.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509). Same 5-set bundle shape (outer zip holding evidence-01..05.zip) as the GM-verified auto-mastery cases; the solver answered 3075/3075 but the attempt EXPIRED (submit RemoteDisconnected after 5.42 s solve on Homebase). expected.json = solver output, spot-checked against the raw bytes (PS base64 record, VBS Codec_ field, While/if-errorlevel control). Speed fix: sets solve in forked processes (group_by_set + ProcessPoolExecutor, sequential fallback); the nested-zip index no longer decrypts every script leaf just to sniff for PK (375 PBKDF2s -> only the members asked, per set, in parallel); powerplay.xor is one big-int XOR.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D23). It is permanently UNVERIFIABLE: the D8 attempt EXPIRED (no GM grade), so expected.json stays {}. The D23 solver's 3075 answers equal the D8 solver's submission (mastery/attempts/7432c509/last_timed.json) 3075/3075, and that solver's other D8 Mastery runs were GM-verified. The real failure was SPEED: ~1100 serial AES PBKDF2s on the D23 Mastery bundle. Rule: the WinZip-AES salt sits right after each local header, so derive every member key up front in a thread pool (OpenSSL PBKDF2 releases the GIL) and look it up in the pyzipper PBKDF2 patch (solve.py `_prefetch_keys`/`_KEYS`). Profiled time dropped from 9.8 s to 5.1 s on auto-mastery-e24c070d; answers unchanged.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D24 -- still UNVERIFIABLE, expected.json {}; solver fills 3075/3075, the same answers as before this change). The real failure was SPEED on the single (2.61 s under load against a 2.5 s budget). Rule: a one-set contract now splits its questions BY ARTIFACT across forked workers (k = min(#artifacts, CPUs in the affinity mask, 4); same ProcessPoolExecutor path as Mastery sets, sequential fallback, output re-sorted into contract order), and the node JScript decode runs in a thread that overlaps the Python analysis (joined only when the first JS member is analysed). Single: 1.4 s -> 0.6 s (12 CPU), 1.0 s with `taskset -c 0,1`; answers identical to the sequential run (1516/1516 GM-verified).\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D25 -- still UNVERIFIABLE, expected.json {}; solver fills 3075/3075 in 0.66 s, single 1571/1571 GM-verified in 0.38 s, 0.4-0.5 s pinned to 2 CPUs). The flagged 2.59 s single / in-window timeout was measured under host load (loadavg ~9 with parallel generalize workers): sequential cProfile is 0.73 s, of which ~0.2 s is 341 module-level `re.compile`s in `lib.codec` + 88 in `lib.warmups_codecs` and ~0.25 s `lib.codec.peel_plan` for the bash members (already the fast path vs the ~60 ms/file interpreter). No solver change; the remaining wins are lib-side (below).\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- `lib.codec` / `lib.warmups_codecs` (D25 re-measure): 341 + 88 regexes compiled at import = ~0.2 s of a 0.73 s sequential single. Compile lazily (e.g. a `functools.cache`d `_re(name)` accessor) -- it is now the biggest fixed cost of every script-lane solver.\n- `lib.codec` import is ~0.3 s, almost all of it from ~340 module-level `re.compile`s (cProfile, D24 single). Compiling lazily would save that on every script-lane solver start.\n- Solver skeleton: when `group_by_set` gives one group, split the questions by artifact across forked workers (independent members), like D24 solve.py `main()`.\n- `lib.common.gm_error_body(artdir) -> bytes|None`: detect a GM error JSON (`rate_limited`, ...) saved in place of an\n  artifact, so every solver fails loudly. Better still: the harness download step (hctf/hb_timed.py) should retry on\n  `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` and never save it as the artifact. That would have saved this timed attempt.\n- `lib.common.iter_entries(root, leaf_ext=...)`: don't load a member just to sniff for a nested zip when its name has a script/text extension (.js/.ps1/.py/.vbs/.cmd/.bat/.sh/.mjs/.php/...). Today it decrypts every AES member of a Mastery bundle up front (375 PBKDF2 = ~0.2 s locally, many times that on Homebase), and each forked set repeats it. See `_iter_entries` in this solve.py.\n- `lib.common._zip_open`: prefetch WinZip-AES keys for all members in parallel (salt = bytes after the local header; `hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac` in a ThreadPoolExecutor), as D23 solve.py `_prefetch_keys` does. Every script-lane Mastery solver pays ~1 ms x members serially today.\n- `lib.codec`: ~295 module-level `re.compile`s cost ~0.3 s per solver start, and `_find_codecs_uncached` runs ~16 regex probes per text (4669 searches / 287 texts = 0.4 s on the D23 Mastery). Compile lazily and gate each detector on a cheap substring test (`'Codec_' in text`, ...).\n{\n  \"70eb6188-68e0-41da-8044-23f8f3359ab1\": {\n    \"resource-scenario\": \"resource_hijacking\",\n    \"resource-attack\": \"T1496\",\n    \"resource-url\": \"https://cdn-fac731b54f.example.invalid/a5881cf54f55.bin\",\n    \"resource-wallet\": \"wallet_dbbf35406b28a16edd9d3402946f83c7\",\n    \"resource-mutex\": \"Global\\\\HuntressLab_6b90e906a4a3\",\n    \"collection-scenario\": \"synthetic_collection\",\n    \"collection-attack\": \"T1555.003\",\n    \"collection-url\": \"https://cdn-27ca567aa1.example.invalid/16a153a3b9bf.bin\",\n    \"collection-file\": \"update-27ca567aa1.dat\",\n    \"collection-user-agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0 HuntressLab/9.2\",\n    \"impact-scenario\": \"synthetic_impact\",\n    \"impact-attack\": \"T1486\",\n    \"impact-extension\": \".locked-ab350\",\n    \"impact-note\": \"RECOVERY-ab350db338.txt\",\n    \"impact-url\": \"https://ab350db338.example.invalid/2e76cbb5cc0a.dat\",\n    \"jscript-benign-role\": \"benign_utility\",\n    \"jscript-benign-attack\": \"none\",\n    \"jscript-benign-adapter\": \"Scripting.Dictionary\",\n    \"jscript-benign-campaign\": \"campaign-9731d3780d\",\n    \"jscript-benign-host\": \"host-9731d3780d\",\n    \"jscript-stager-role\": \"stager\",\n    \"jscript-stager-attack\": \"T1105\",\n    \"jscript-stager-adapter\": \"Microsoft.XMLHTTP\",\n    \"jscript-stager-campaign\": \"campaign-7c249c589a\",\n    \"jscript-stager-url\": \"https://7c249c589a.example.invalid/34d88c4b.dat\",\n    \"jscript-dropper-role\": \"dropper\",\n    \"jsc